Q: How to write a &quot;where not in&quot; query with nested &quot;select&quot; using Query Builder Class A: <p>This will be the solution if you're strictly looking for <code>CodeIgniter query</code> but if you don't mind mingling it with normal SQL queries then @Vickel's answer should do the deed.
<br></p>

<pre><code>//first get the data you want to ignore-
$not_in_arr = $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('product_id')-&gt;from('purchases')-&gt;get()-&gt;result();
</code></pre>

<p><code>where_in</code> and <code>where_not_in</code> expect you to pass an indexed array as the 2nd parameter.<br></p>

<pre><code>//get the data in indexed array format-
foreach($not_in_arr as $not){
    $notin[] = $not-&gt;product_id;
}

$query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('*')-&gt;from('products')-&gt;where_not_in('id', $notin)-&gt;get()-&gt;result();

echo $this-&gt;db-&gt;last_query(); //returns- SELECT * FROM `products` WHERE `id` NOT IN('1', '2', '3')
</code></pre>

<p>Hope this helps you. :) </p>

Q: SELECT rows from a table WHERE the id is not found in a second table using CodeIgniter&#39;s query builder A: <pre><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;select('*');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;from('pegawai');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('user', 'user.id_pegawai = pegawai.id_pegawai', 'left');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;where('user.id_pegawai is null');
$query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get();
return $query-&gt;result();
</code></pre>

<p>Found it already :D</p>

Q: How to append a LIMIT clause to a raw MySQL query in CodeIgniter A: <p>It is a strange choice to manually build a database query within a framework which deliberately designed a query builder design pattern. Let's save some future brain cells by putting you on an easier development path.</p>
<p>The following uses easy-to-follow parenthetical grouping and calls only <code>get_where()</code> to consolidate <code>from()</code>, <code>where()</code>, <code>limit()</code>, and <code>get()</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getArticles(string $search_key, int $pub_status, ?int $limit = 10): array
{
    if ($pub_status === 0) {
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;where('private_status &lt;&gt;', 1);
    }

    if ($search_key) {
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;group_start();
        foreach (explode(' ', $search_key) as $word) {
            $this-&gt;db
                -&gt;or_like([
                    'article_title' =&gt; $word,
                    'article_abstract' =&gt; $word,
                    'article_keywords' =&gt; $word,
                ]);
        }
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;group_end();
    }

    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;get_where('ms_article', ['article_status' =&gt; 1], $limit)
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Now, I can't fathom a reason to avoid the query builder features, but if you wanted to reinvent that wheel, you should escape the $word strings properly so that the LIKE conditions are not hackable and a parameterized statement should be used.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getArticles(string $search_key, int $pub_status, ?int $limit = 10): array
{
    $sql = &quot;
        SELECT *
        FROM ms_article
        WHERE article_status = ?
    &quot;;

    $params = [1];

    if ($pub_status === 0) {
        $sql .= &quot; AND private_status &lt;&gt; ? &quot;;
        $params[] = 1;
    }

    if ($search_key) {
        $sql .= &quot; AND (&quot;;
        $orParts = [];
        foreach (explode(' ', $search_key) as $word) {
            $orParts[] = &quot;
                article_title LIKE ? ESCAPE '!'
                OR article_abstract LIKE ? ESCAPE '!'
                OR article_keywords LIKE ? ESCAPE '!'
            &quot;;

            $like = '%' . strtr($word, ['!' =&gt; '!!', '%' =&gt; '!%', '_' =&gt; '!_']) . '%';
            array_push($params, $like, $like, $like);
        }
        $sql .= implode(' OR ', $orParts) . &quot;)&quot;;
    }

    if ($limit !== null) {
        $sql .= &quot; LIMIT $limit&quot;;
    }

    return $this-&gt;db-&gt;query($sql, $params)-&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>strtr()</code> call, above, is effectively mimicking CodeIgniter's <code>escape_like_str()</code> using hardcoded character replacements.</p>
